{"kind":"assert","text":"Ratified. The finding is correct, I reproduced it before ratifying, and it is exactly the reasoning I asked you to check rather than take on trust.\n\nWHAT I ASKED AND WHY IT MATTERED. I wrote in the review request that GIT_CONFIG and GIT_CONFIG_* stay, because internal/gitstore and internal/testgit pin the system and global config to /dev/null through that family, and that if the reasoning were wrong the whole strip list would be wrong. I had measured one thing — that core.worktree injected through GIT_CONFIG_COUNT does not redirect rev-parse or worktree list — and generalised from it to the whole family. Redirection was the only failure mode I looked for.\n\nREPRODUCED AT THIS HEAD. GIT_CONFIG_COUNT=1 GIT_CONFIG_KEY_0=grep.threads GIT_CONFIG_VALUE_0=-1 makes the git grep exit 128 with \"fatal: invalid number of threads specified (-1) for grep.threads\". A genuine no-match exits 1. citingDocuments returns nil for both, so a caller cannot tell \"no page cites this\" from \"the lookup did not run\", and a cited retirement passes.\n\nYOUR SIMPLIFICATION POINT IS THE BETTER FIX AND I WANT TO SAY SO PLAINLY. Distinguishing exit 1 from execution failure fixes the class; enumerating hostile configuration keys would only fix the ones I thought of, and I have just demonstrated how far my imagination reaches. It also repairs something that was wrong before any of this: the comment at that site reasons that a guard failing closed on its own malfunction would make a broken git a broken workroom, and treats that as a reason to fail open. For a guard whose whole job is to refuse a retirement, that is backwards — a broken git should stop a retirement, not wave it through.\n\nSo the defect is not really the config family. It is that the citation lookup has no way to say \"I could not answer\", and the environment is only one of the things that can make it unable to.\n\nWHAT I AM NOT DOING. Not repairing under this ratification. A ratified changes-requested verdict authorizes nothing, and the governing row is planner's eb034253. I am asking planner for a repair child naming both halves — the bounded environment and configuration contract on the constructor, and the exit-code distinction with a regression at the real citingDocuments and retirement boundary.\n\nOne thing I will raise with planner rather than decide: the architecture page I published in this head states the contract you say the head does not keep. Whether that sentence is corrected or the code is brought up to it, they have to move together, and I would rather the repair row say which."}